<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Posts - 0make</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/</link><description>This is my new Hugo FixIt site</description><generator>Hugo 0.168.0 &amp; FixIt v0.4.5</generator><language>en</language><managingEditor>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</managingEditor><webMaster>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:42:54 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://0make.top/posts/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Transformer LLM vs Poker AI: Two Different Tech Trees in Deep Learning</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/transformer-vs-poker-ai/</link><pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/transformer-vs-poker-ai/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;p&gt;They are both products of &lt;strong&gt;Deep Learning&lt;/strong&gt;, relying on the same underlying principles we discussed earlier—Deep Neural Networks (DNN), gradient descent—and ultimately saving weight parameter files. However, their &lt;strong&gt;brain structures (architectures)&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;learning methods (training paradigms)&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;specialized tasks (objectives)&lt;/strong&gt; are worlds apart.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>What Exactly Is a Trained Model File? Demystifying AI's "Brain Memory Backup"</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/model-weights-explained/</link><pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/model-weights-explained/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="introduction"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#introduction" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;If you&amp;rsquo;ve ever trained a deep learning model, you&amp;rsquo;ve definitely encountered files with extensions like &lt;code&gt;.pth&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.h5&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;.onnx&lt;/code&gt;. They might be only tens of megabytes or several gigabytes in size. But have you ever wondered: &lt;strong&gt;what exactly is inside these files?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Deep Monte-Carlo Algorithm (DMC): From Self-Play to Intuitive Decision-Making</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/deep-monte-carlo/</link><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/deep-monte-carlo/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="introduction"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#introduction" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the field of artificial intelligence, game playing has always been one of the most challenging problems. From Go to poker, different types of games impose vastly different requirements on algorithms. Today, we will delve into the &lt;strong&gt;Deep Monte-Carlo Algorithm (DMC)&lt;/strong&gt;, an innovative method that has demonstrated remarkable power in imperfect information games.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Deep Neural Network (DNN) Principles: From Neurons to 'Intuitive' Decision-Making</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/deep-neural-network/</link><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/deep-neural-network/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="introduction"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#introduction" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;When we talk about artificial intelligence, &amp;ldquo;Deep Neural Network&amp;rdquo; (DNN) is undoubtedly one of the most core technologies. But is its principle really that mysterious?&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>The Mathematical Essence of Deep Neural Networks: From Composite Functions to Gradient Descent</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/dnn-mathematics/</link><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/dnn-mathematics/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="introduction"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#introduction" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;When we marvel at the amazing capabilities of artificial intelligence, we rarely think about the mathematical foundation behind it. In fact, &lt;strong&gt;the essence of Deep Neural Networks (DNN), mathematically, is an extremely complex composite function&lt;/strong&gt;. It fits the mapping relationship between input data and output results through nested layers of simple linear transformations and non-linear activation functions.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Ding Ge Dan AI Algorithm Design: An Innovative Approach Combining Combinatorial Optimization and Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/dinggedan/</link><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 08:30:00 +0800</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/dinggedan/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="introduction"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#introduction" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;Ding Ge Dan&amp;rdquo; is a highly distinctive local poker game popular in the Fanshi region of Shanxi Province. Although it also involves 4 players, two decks (108 cards), and 2v2 competition, its unique &amp;ldquo;card exchange mechanism&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;counter-intuitive card rankings&amp;rdquo; make it fundamentally different from games like Guandan and Dou Dizhu.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;This article provides a detailed introduction to the AI algorithm solution specifically designed for &amp;ldquo;Ding Ge Dan&amp;rdquo;, which integrates various advanced technologies including combinatorial optimization, multi-agent reinforcement learning, and Monte Carlo Tree Search.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Supervised Learning</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/sl/</link><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/sl/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Supervised Learning is one of the three core paradigms of machine learning (the other two being unsupervised learning and reinforcement learning). It is currently the most widely used and mature machine learning method.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="-what-is-supervised-learning"&gt;&lt;span&gt;🎓 What is Supervised Learning?&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#-what-is-supervised-learning" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Supervised Learning&lt;/strong&gt; is a machine learning method that uses &lt;strong&gt;labeled datasets&lt;/strong&gt; to train algorithms, allowing the model to learn the mapping relationship between inputs and outputs.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Unsupervised Learning</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/usl/</link><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/usl/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Unsupervised Learning is another core paradigm of machine learning, forming a sharp contrast with supervised learning.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="what-is-unsupervised-learning"&gt;&lt;span&gt;What is Unsupervised Learning?&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#what-is-unsupervised-learning" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h3 class="heading-element" id="-one-sentence-definition"&gt;&lt;span&gt;📝 One-Sentence Definition&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#-one-sentence-definition" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&#10;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Unsupervised Learning&lt;/strong&gt; is a machine learning method that discovers hidden patterns, structures, or relationships in &lt;strong&gt;unlabeled data&lt;/strong&gt; without requiring人工标注.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Reinforcement Learning (RL) Development History</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/rl/</link><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0800</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/rl/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/ai/">AI</category><description>&lt;p&gt;Reinforcement Learning (RL) is one of the three core paradigms of machine learning (alongside supervised learning and unsupervised learning).&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Its core idea is: &lt;strong&gt;An agent learns the optimal policy through trial and error by interacting with the environment, based on reward or penalty signals&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Throughout the history of AI development, reinforcement learning has undergone several major paradigm shifts, evolving from early mathematical theoretical derivations to becoming the core engine driving large language models and embodied AI today.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>About Me</title><link>https://0make.top/posts/me/</link><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 01:24:45 +0000</pubDate><author>0make@gmail.com (AZ)</author><guid>https://0make.top/posts/me/</guid><category domain="https://0make.top/categories/about/me/">About/Me</category><description>&lt;h2 class="heading-element" id="past-projects"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Past Projects&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="#past-projects" class="heading-mark"&gt;&#10; &lt;svg class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.